(project pricklypear-soil
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: SOIL — the native/Wayland tier. Pads grow in soil: a shim process buys the frame loop (Wayland compositing, layout, pixels) while the image owns all policy in Nopales. Build order: River as the compositor (tier 2), filmstrip's two-sequence workspace as the policy, Clay as the layout engine, pads/render-scene as the view dialect. Full compositor (tier 3) is explicitly out of scope until the policy layer is proven.")
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-09 DESIGN SESSION (with pads.borg, filmstrip.borg): the tier ladder from the lisp-machine conversation — tier 1 filmstrip-in-browser (product), tier 2 Nopales-as-River-WM (the rau/mtwm architecture: C compositor, Lisp policy over the wire), tier 3 full compositor C shim + eval listeners (the mahogany/lispwc architecture) — with tier 2 fixed as THE chapter scope and tier 3 deferred. Precedents named in-session: rau (pure elisp WM on River), mtwm (Common Lisp on River, WIP), mahogany (CL on wlroots, C backend split), lispwc (wl_listener as Lisp closure workout), StumpWM issue #739 (why you never port an X11 WM to Wayland). ~/dev/clay_test holds clay.h 0.14 — the pun that named the chapter: prickly pears grow in clay soil; pads lower into Clay.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-04 MAKE (soil M-A, branch filmstrip-v1): the kiosk milestone shipped — soil-hello renders (filmstrip/scene) fullscreen on the running compositor (scripts/smoke_soil_ma.py green: scene validation over live workspace mutations + --frames 12 exit 0). Shipped in this pass: filmstrip/scene (workspace -> pad tree -> render-scene, in libs/filmstrip), niri width presets as grow weights 2/3/6 over denominator 6 with a trailing spacer for exact fractions, sel/dim pass-through display-hint attrs (scene-attrs already serialized them; shim paints accent color + 2px border). Binding-path decision (a) thin shim now PROVEN twice (hello card + filmstrip shell); native-rendering implemented; river-split + milestones stay planned — river-window-management is M-B, and M-A ran on plain Hyprland, no river needed yet. Scene dialect stayed v0: no new kinds, hints not paint in the tree.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)

 (section soil-contract
  (doc "One rule, load-bearing: BUY the frame loop, OWN the policy.
      Composite, layout, measure, and repaint are C/host work at frame
      frequency; window policy, shell state, and every user-level
      decision are Nopales at event frequency (10-100x/sec worst case,
      well inside image-eval budget). No Nopales eval ever runs on a
      frame path — the pads/renderer-dialects latency non-goal is this
      chapter's contract. The shim is the thinnest host that can
      observe Wayland events, serve vsoil prims, and apply policy
      replies; the image is the truth.")

  (subsection river-split
   (doc "Tier 2 rides River the way rau and mtwm do: River (wlroots)
       owns outputs, input, and compositing; the window-management
       POLICY is a separate client speaking river-window-management-v1
       over the Wayland socket. Filmstrip's workspace model lands
       naturally: the content strip is the layout the policy proposes
       on manage-start; the cascade is shell UI, not a window concern.
       Manage events (map/unmap/focus/output) marshal IN to Nopales
       evals; layout decisions marshal OUT as data. Dev loop never
       leaves the current desktop: run river NESTED inside Hyprland and
       attach the shim to the nested socket.")
   (details
    (doc (|
         WHY RIVER AND NOT wlroots-DIRECT: wlroots' API churn is a
         known cost center (mahogany pins exact minors; lispwc calls
         itself a workout, not a toolkit). River amortizes compositor
         correctness (the ~50k lines wlroots says you were going to
         write anyway, plus the event-loop scars StumpWM #739
         catalogued) behind one small, window-management-shaped
         protocol. The price: River's opinions are the ceiling —
         acceptable because filmstrip IS a strip; niri-ish columns are
         close to River's native mental model.
    |))
   )
   (status planned)
  )

  (subsection binding-path
   (doc "Bindings: talex5/ocaml-wayland generates clients from protocol
       XML and is Eio/direct-style — aligned with the runtime effects
       transition (docs/runtime-effects-transition.md), so soil lands
       ON the eio floor rather than beside it. Two eval topologies,
       decision deferred to make: (a) THIN shim — host talks /api/eval
       over loopback; nothing new in the image; latency paid per
       policy event (~ms; fine at event frequency); (b) EMBEDDED shim —
       the pure interpreter (lisp/lib, DB-free by design) runs
       IN-process against local defs synced pp-sync-style, with the
       image as object store; faster, but forks the session story.
       Default (a); measure; promote (b) only if policy latency is
       felt. Security follows the REPL rule: loopback only, no
       application auth in the shim channel v1.")
   (status implemented)
  )

  (subsection native-rendering
   (doc "The shell's own pixels: pads/render-scene lowers the workspace
       tree to a flat (rect text style) op list; the shim feeds those
       boxes to Clay for layout (fit/grow/fixed + row/column/flow is
       Clay's exact feature list — no impedance layer) and renders with
       cairo/pango, the mahogany backend pairing. Text stays
       renderer-measured per the pads text-runs rule: the shim owns the
       measure callback. v1 damage discipline is intentionally dumb —
       kiosk-sized repaint on change; culling/occlusion/motion damage
       are v2 concerns, stated so nobody gold-plates the blitter.")
   (implements libs/pads/render-scene.pp)
   (status implemented)
  )

  (subsection milestones
   (doc "Two milestones, separately dogfoodable. M-A KIOSK CLIENT: the
       shim is ONE river client drawing the filmstrip shell fullscreen
       — the terminal/ANSI desktop's native twin; no WM policy needed;
       proves bindings + render-scene + Clay end to end. M-B WINDOW
       POLICY: the shim speaks river-window-management and each pane
       is a REAL window; filmstrip routes foreign apps (foot, browser)
       through the same workspace row as habitat panes; xdg-popup and
       set_modal serve pads/layers-modality unchanged — the spec's z-
       band/modal mappings were written for exactly this. Tier 3 (own
       compositor, wl_listener-per-closure) stays OUT of scope here:
       reconsider only when M-B has been a daily driver long enough to
       chafe at River's ceiling.")
   (verify
    (smoke "M-A: nested river shows the filmstrip shell fullscreen from pads/render-scene + owned layout")
    (smoke "M-B: foot maps into the strip, alt+arrows focus it, close event removes the pane record")
    (test "policy latency: p95 manage-event -> layout-reply eval under event budget, measured on the thin shim"))
   (status planned)
  )
 )
)
